    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(3))  # F0..F2\n    customers = list(range(10))  # C0..C9\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(96, 72), (17, 37), (34, 96)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(51, 28), (27, 0), (43, 11), (57, 13), (39, 16), (93, 45), (28, 45), (76, 77), (55, 14), (89, 42)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [140.0, 121.0, 189.0]\n    customer_demands = [45.0, 25.0, 28.0, 14.0, 12.0, 40.0, 35.0, 45.0, 24.0, 13.0]\n    fixed_costs = [318.0, 139.0, 126.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.74  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "We need to decide which warehouses to open and how to serve our customers in the most cost-effective way. Our short list of candidate sites is mapped in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (96, 72)\n  - F1: (17, 37)\n  - F2: (34, 96), and our customer locations are summarized in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (51, 28)\n  - C1: (27, 0)\n  - C2: (43, 11)\n  - C3: (57, 13)\n  - C4: (39, 16)\n  - C5: (93, 45)\n  - C6: (28, 45)\n  - C7: (76, 77)\n  - C8: (55, 14)\n  - C9: (89, 42). Opening a site triggers a fixed expense, detailed in [318.0, 139.0, 126.0], and a facility can only ship if it\u2019s open.\n\nShipping costs will be driven by how far each warehouse is from each customer and by the quantity we move, using 0.74. At the same time, each facility has limited capacity, as outlined in [140.0, 121.0, 189.0], and every customer\u2019s demand must be met in full, as shown in [45.0, 25.0, 28.0, 14.0, 12.0, 40.0, 35.0, 45.0, 24.0, 13.0].\n\nThe decision in front of us is to balance where we open warehouses and how we route orders so we minimize total cost\u2014the sum of opening costs plus transportation spend\u2014while staying within each site\u2019s limits and fully serving all customers. Opening more sites generally reduces delivery distances and may lower shipping expense, but increases fixed costs. Opening fewer sites reduces fixed costs, but may require longer hauls and can strain capacity. Our plan is to evaluate which facilities to activate and how to allocate customer orders to those facilities, with the goal of achieving the lowest overall cost under these business rules.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse opening and shipping plan \u2014 selecting the right sites at the lowest total cost\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to decide which warehouses to open and how to route product to customers for the upcoming planning horizon. We have three candidate warehouse locations and ten customer delivery points. The core challenge is to balance the one-time expense of opening sites against the ongoing expense of moving product, while making sure every customer is fully served and no facility is over-committed.\n\nWhat we know\n- Candidate warehouse locations are provided as map coordinates in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}.\n- Customer delivery locations are provided as map coordinates in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}.\n- Opening each warehouse triggers a fixed cost, detailed in {FIXED_COSTS}.\n- Shipping costs scale with how far we move product and how much we ship; the transport rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE}.\n- Each warehouse can handle only so much volume; those limits are in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}.\n- Each customer has a specific demand that must be met in full; those requirements are listed in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n\nKey business rules\n- We can only ship from locations we decide to open.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be completely satisfied.\n- No warehouse may exceed its available capacity.\n\nDecision to make\n- Which of the candidate warehouses to open.\n- How much product to ship from each open warehouse to each customer, reflecting the trade-off between proximity and volume.\n\nWhat success looks like\n- We minimize the combined cost of opening warehouses and shipping product, while honoring capacity limits and fulfilling every order. In practice, this means weighing a lower fixed-cost site that\u2019s farther from many customers against a more centrally located site that may cost more to open but shortens delivery distances. In some cases, capacity limits may require opening more than one site even if one location appears cheaper on paper.\n\nNext steps\n- Using the location, capacity, demand, and cost inputs above, we will evaluate feasible combinations of sites and shipment plans, and recommend an operating configuration that meets all demand at the lowest total cost."
}